\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The object of study}

Egyptian fraction representations --- sums of distinct reciprocals of positive integers ---
are among the oldest surviving mathematical formalisms, and remain a persistent source of
hard elementary problems. The particular object studied here is the exact decomposition of
unity.

\begin{definition}[Exact Egyptian covering]\label{def:cov}
A finite set $S \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is an \emph{exact Egyptian covering}, or simply a
\emph{covering}, if every $n \in S$ satisfies $n \ge 2$ and
\[
  \sum_{n \in S} \frac{1}{n} = 1 .
\]
\end{definition}

Because $S$ is a set, its denominators are automatically distinct; the condition $n \ge 2$
excludes the degenerate representation $1 = 1/1$ and any appeal to $1/0$. The archetype is
$1 = \tfrac12 + \tfrac13 + \tfrac16$, i.e.\ $S = \{2,3,6\}$.

\begin{definition}[Covering-free set]
A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is \emph{covering-free} if no finite subset of $A$ is an
exact covering.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Divergent reciprocals]\label{def:div}
A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ \emph{has divergent reciprocals} if for every rational $M$
there is a finite $F \subseteq A$ with $\sum_{n\in F} 1/n > M$.
\end{definition}

Divergence is phrased in this finite-partial-sum form deliberately: it is a purely rational,
purely combinatorial condition, and the whole development below stays inside $\mathbb{Q}$.

\begin{definition}[The colouring property]
For $r \in \mathbb{N}$, the \emph{Erd\H{o}s--Graham property for $r$ colours}, written
$\EG(r)$, asserts: for every map $c$ from the integers to a palette of $r$ colours there
exist an exact covering $S$ and a colour $i$ with $c(n) = i$ for all $n \in S$. The $i$-th
\emph{colour class} is $C_i = \{ n \ge 2 : c(n) = i\}$.
\end{definition}

The \emph{Erd\H{o}s--Graham conjecture} asserts that $\EG(r)$ holds for every finite $r$; it
was proved by Croot (2003) using estimates on smooth numbers. Our aim is orthogonal: to
describe the combinatorial structure of the objects involved, to prove the elementary parts
unconditionally, and to identify precisely which obstructions rule out the elementary routes.

\section{The structure of exact coverings}

\begin{theorem}[Bracketing]\label{thm:bracket}
Let $S$ be an exact covering. Then $S \neq \varnothing$ and
\begin{enumerate}[label=(\arabic*)]
\item there exists $n \in S$ with $n \le |S|$;
\item there exists $n' \in S$ with $n' \ge |S|$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Nonemptiness is immediate, the empty sum being $0 \ne 1$. For (1), let $m = \min S \ge 2$;
then $1/n \le 1/m$ for all $n \in S$, so $1 = \sum_{n \in S} 1/n \le |S|/m$, giving
$m \le |S|$. For (2), let $M = \max S$; then $1/n \ge 1/M$ for all $n \in S$, so
$1 \ge |S|/M$, giving $M \ge |S|$.
\end{proof}

A useful consequence: a covering whose denominators all exceed $K$ must have more than $K$
elements.

\begin{theorem}[Minimum size]\label{thm:three}
Every exact covering has at least three elements.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
If $|S|=1$, say $S = \{a\}$ with $a \ge 2$, then $1/a = 1$ forces $a = 1$, a contradiction.
If $|S| = 2$, say $S = \{a,b\}$ with $a \ne b$ and both $\ge 2$, then after relabelling
$b \ge 3$, whence
\[
  \frac1a + \frac1b \le \frac12 + \frac13 = \frac56 < 1,
\]
contradicting $\sum_{n\in S}1/n = 1$.
\end{proof}

Note that distinctness is what forces the larger denominator to be at least $3$.

\begin{theorem}[Classification in size three]\label{thm:unique}
If $S$ is an exact covering with $|S| = 3$, then $S = \{2,3,6\}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Write $S = \{a,b,c\}$ with $2 \le a < b < c$ and $1/a+1/b+1/c = 1$.
Since $1/b, 1/c < 1/a$ we get $1 < 3/a$, so $a < 3$ and hence $a = 2$.
Then $1/b + 1/c = 1/2$ with $1/c < 1/b$, so $1/2 < 2/b$, giving $b < 4$; as $b > 2$ we get
$b = 3$. Finally $1/c = 1/2 - 1/3 = 1/6$, so $c = 6$. An unordered triple reduces to this
sorted case by trichotomy.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Splitting identity]\label{lem:split}
For every $m \ge 1$, \quad $\dfrac1m = \dfrac{1}{m+1} + \dfrac{1}{m(m+1)}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
$\frac{1}{m+1}+\frac{1}{m(m+1)} = \frac{m+1}{m(m+1)} = \frac1m$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Splitting operator]\label{thm:splitop}
Let $S$ be an exact covering with maximum $m$, and set
\[
  T = \bigl(S\setminus\{m\}\bigr) \cup \{\, m+1,\ m(m+1) \,\}.
\]
Then $T$ is an exact covering, $|T| = |S| + 1$, and $\max T = m(m+1)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Since $m \ge 2$ we have $m < m+1 < m(m+1)$, so both new elements strictly exceed
$\max S = m$; they are distinct from each other and absent from $S\setminus\{m\}$, whence
$|T| = (|S|-1)+2 = |S|+1$. All members of $T$ are $\ge 2$. Writing
$\Sigma' = \sum_{n \in S\setminus\{m\}} 1/n = 1 - 1/m$, Lemma~\ref{lem:split} gives
\[
  \sum_{n \in T}\frac1n = \frac{1}{m+1} + \frac{1}{m(m+1)} + \Sigma'
  = \frac1m + \Bigl(1 - \frac1m\Bigr) = 1 .
\]
Every inherited element is $\le m < m(m+1)$ and $m+1 < m(m+1)$, so $\max T = m(m+1)$.
\end{proof}

The maximality hypothesis is essential: splitting a non-maximal element may reintroduce a
denominator already present, and the set structure would silently absorb the duplicate,
destroying the sum. Because the operator returns a covering with a \emph{known} maximum, it
iterates indefinitely.

\begin{theorem}[Existence in every size $\ge 3$]\label{thm:exists}
For every $k \ge 3$ there is an exact covering with exactly $k$ elements.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By induction on $j$ we produce a covering $S_j$ of size $j+3$ possessing a maximum. Base:
$S_0 = \{2,3,6\}$ with maximum $6$. Step: apply Theorem~\ref{thm:splitop} to $S_j$ and its
maximum. Take $j = k-3$.
\end{proof}

The resulting ladder is
$\{2,3,6\},\ \{2,3,7,42\},\ \{2,3,7,43,1806\},\ \{2,3,7,43,1807,3263442\},\dots$,
whose maxima follow Sylvester's sequence.

\begin{theorem}[Cardinality spectrum]\label{thm:spectrum}
There exists an exact covering of cardinality $k$ if and only if $k \ge 3$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Combine Theorems~\ref{thm:three} and~\ref{thm:exists}.
\end{proof}

\section{The pigeonhole step}

\begin{theorem}[Dyadic harmonic lower bound]\label{thm:dyadic}
For every $k \ge 0$, \quad $\displaystyle \sum_{n=2}^{2^{k}} \frac1n \ \ge\ \frac k2$,
as an inequality of rational numbers.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Induction on $k$. For $k=0$ the sum is empty and the bound is $0$. Assuming the bound for
$k$, split off the block $2^k < n \le 2^{k+1}$: it has $2^k$ terms, each at least
$1/2^{k+1}$, hence contributes at least $1/2$. Adding gives $(k+1)/2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:divall}
The set $\{n : n\ge 2\}$ has divergent reciprocals: given $M$, choose $k > 2M+1$ and take
$F = \{2,\dots,2^k\}$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Some colour class is reciprocally large]\label{thm:pigeon}
For every $r$ and every $r$-colouring $c$ of the integers $\ge 2$, some colour class $C_i$
has divergent reciprocals.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Otherwise each class $C_i$ admits a bound $M_i$ on the reciprocal sums of its finite
subsets. Put $M = \sum_i M_i$. By Corollary~\ref{cor:divall} pick a finite $F$ of integers
$\ge 2$ with $\sum_{n\in F}1/n > M$. Partitioning $F$ into fibres $F_i$ and summing,
$\sum_{n\in F} 1/n = \sum_i \sum_{n\in F_i} 1/n \le \sum_i M_i = M$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Finiteness of the palette enters only through the finite sum $\sum_i M_i$.

\section{The local obstruction}

Let $v_p(n)$ denote the $p$-adic valuation, extended to rationals by
$v_p(x/y) = v_p(x)-v_p(y)$. Recall the ultrametric inequality
$v_p(x+y) \ge \min(v_p(x),v_p(y))$, with equality whenever $v_p(x) \ne v_p(y)$.

\begin{theorem}[Local obstruction]\label{thm:local}
Let $S$ be an exact covering, $p$ a prime, and $m \in S$ with $v_p(m) > 0$. Then there is
$n \in S$ with $n \ne m$ and $v_p(n) \ge v_p(m)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Suppose $v_p(n) < v_p(m) =: e \ge 1$ for all $n \in S\setminus\{m\}$. Since
$v_p(1/n) = -v_p(n)$, the term $1/m$ has valuation $-e$ and every other term has valuation
$> -e$; the minimum is attained uniquely. By the equality case of the ultrametric
inequality, applied inductively over the terms,
\[
  v_p\Bigl(\sum_{n \in S}\frac1n\Bigr) = -e < 0,
\]
whereas the sum equals $1$ and $v_p(1) = 0$.
\end{proof}

Equivalently: in an exact covering the maximal power of any prime dividing a denominator is
attained at least twice. In $\{2,3,6\}$ the maximum $v_2 = 1$ occurs at $2$ and $6$, and
$v_3 = 1$ at $3$ and $6$.

\begin{corollary}[Pairing]\label{cor:pair}
For every prime $p$ and every exact covering $S$, the number of elements of $S$ divisible
by $p$ is never exactly $1$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
If $m$ were the unique multiple of $p$ then $v_p(m) \ge 1$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:local}
gives $n \ne m$ in $S$ with $v_p(n) \ge 1$, i.e.\ $p \mid n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
An exact covering never contains exactly one even number.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}
A set $A \subseteq \mathbb{N}$ is \emph{$p$-adically separated} if for every prime $p$ and
all distinct $m,n \in A$, $v_p(m) = v_p(n)$ forces $v_p(m) = 0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Separation criterion]\label{thm:sep}
Every $p$-adically separated set is covering-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Let $S \subseteq A$ be an exact covering. Pick $x \in S$; as $x \ge 2$, some prime $p$
divides $x$, so $v_p(x) \ge 1$. Let $m \in S$ maximise $v_p$; then $v_p(m) > 0$. By
Theorem~\ref{thm:local} there is $n \ne m$ in $S$ with $v_p(n) \ge v_p(m)$, and maximality
gives $v_p(n) = v_p(m) > 0$ --- contradicting separation.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}
A pairwise coprime family of integers is $p$-adically separated.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If distinct $m,n$ had $v_p(m)=v_p(n)>0$ then $p \mid \gcd(m,n) = 1$, impossible.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:primes}
Every pairwise coprime set of integers $\ge 2$ is covering-free; in particular the set of
primes is covering-free.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:pp}
The set of prime powers is covering-free.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Let $x \ne y$ be prime powers with $v_p(x) = v_p(y) > 0$. Then $p$ divides both, and a
prime power divisible by $p$ is a power of $p$; so $x = p^a$, $y = p^b$ with
$a = v_p(x) = v_p(y) = b$, forcing $x=y$. Hence the prime powers are $p$-adically separated
and Theorem~\ref{thm:sep} applies.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Insufficiency of mass]\label{thm:insuff}
There exists a set $A$ of integers $\ge 2$ that has divergent reciprocals and is
covering-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $A$ to be the primes: divergence is Euler's theorem on $\sum_p 1/p$, in the
finite-partial-sum form of Definition~\ref{def:div}, and covering-freeness is
Corollary~\ref{cor:primes}.
\end{proof}

Hence the pigeonhole step of Theorem~\ref{thm:pigeon} cannot by itself prove the
Erd\H{o}s--Graham conjecture: it produces reciprocal largeness, and reciprocal largeness is
provably compatible with the total absence of exact coverings. Any proof must exploit
arithmetic structure of the colour class beyond its size.

\section{Explicit coverings evading the obstructions}

\begin{theorem}[A covering avoiding all prime powers]\label{thm:npp}
The $21$-element set
\[
  \Npp = \{6, 10, 12, 14, 15, 18, 20, 21, 22, 24, 28, 30, 33, 36, 40, 42, 44, 45, 55, 60, 63\}
\]
is an exact covering, and no member of $\Npp$ is a prime power.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Every listed number divides $27720 = 2^3\cdot 3^2\cdot 5\cdot 7\cdot 11$; writing each
$1/n$ as $(27720/n)/27720$ and summing the numerators gives exactly $27720$. Each member has
at least two distinct prime factors (e.g.\ $6 = 2\cdot 3$, $55 = 5 \cdot 11$,
$63 = 3^2\cdot 7$), hence is not a prime power.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:2colpp}
If a $2$-colouring of the integers $\ge 2$ uses colour $0$ only on prime powers, then it
admits a monochromatic exact covering.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
No member of $\Npp$ is a prime power, so none receives colour $0$; with two colours all
receive colour $1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[A covering avoiding all small denominators]\label{thm:ten}
The $23$-element set
\[
  S_{\ge 10} = \{10, 11, 12, 14, 15, 16, 18, 20, 21, 22, 24, 28, 30, 33, 36, 40, 42, 45, 48,
  55, 60, 63, 66\}
\]
is an exact covering with minimum $10$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:2colsmall}
If a $2$-colouring of the integers $\ge 2$ uses colour $0$ only on integers $< 10$, then it
admits a monochromatic exact covering, namely $S_{\ge 10}$ in colour $1$.
\end{theorem}

By Theorem~\ref{thm:bracket} any covering with minimum $\ge 10$ has at least $11$ elements,
so $S_{\ge 10}$ is within about a factor of two of optimal for its constraint.

\section{The global obstruction: duality with pseudoperfect numbers}

\begin{definition}
A positive integer $N$ is \emph{pseudoperfect} (semiperfect) if some set of distinct proper
divisors of $N$ sums to $N$; \emph{perfect} if all proper divisors sum to $N$;
\emph{deficient} if that sum is $< N$; \emph{abundant} if $> N$; and \emph{weird} if it is
abundant but not pseudoperfect.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Divisor duality]\label{thm:duality}
For $N > 0$: $N$ is pseudoperfect if and only if there is an exact covering all of whose
elements divide $N$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Divisor complementation $\delta : d \mapsto N/d$ is an involution on the divisors of $N$,
hence injective on any set of divisors.

($\Rightarrow$) Let $D$ be a set of distinct proper divisors with $\sum_{d\in D}d = N$, and
put $S = \delta(D)$. Dividing by $N$ gives $\sum_{d\in D} d/N = 1$, i.e.\
$\sum_{s \in S} 1/s = 1$, since $d/N = 1/(N/d)$. Each $s = N/d$ divides $N$, and $s \ge 2$
because $d < N$.

($\Leftarrow$) Let $S$ be an exact covering with $s \mid N$ for all $s\in S$, and put
$D = \delta(S)$: distinct divisors of $N$, each proper because $s \ge 2$ gives $N/s < N$.
Multiplying $\sum_{s\in S}1/s = 1$ by $N$ gives $\sum_{d\in D} d = N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
The least common multiple of any exact covering is pseudoperfect. For $\{2,3,6\}$ the lcm is
$6$, which is perfect.
\end{corollary}

\begin{corollary}
Every perfect number carries an exact covering by its divisors:
$1 = \frac16+\frac13+\frac12$ for $N = 6$, and
$1 = \frac1{28}+\frac1{14}+\frac17+\frac14+\frac12$ for $N = 28$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proposition}
Any set $D$ of distinct proper divisors of $N > 0$ with $\sum_{d \in D} d = N$ has
$|D| \ge 3$. (Apply $\delta$ and Theorem~\ref{thm:three}.)
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Mass obstruction]\label{thm:mass}
If $N > 0$ is deficient, then $\{n : n \mid N,\ n \ge 2\}$ is covering-free.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
An exact covering of divisors of $N$ would make $N$ pseudoperfect by
Theorem~\ref{thm:duality}; but then the full proper-divisor sum is at least $N$,
contradicting deficiency.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
For any prime $p$ and any $k$, the divisors of $p^k$ that are $\ge 2$ form a covering-free
set, since $\sum_{i<k}p^i = (p^k-1)/(p-1) < p^k$.
\end{corollary}

This re-derives part of Corollary~\ref{cor:pp} by a wholly different route. The two
obstructions are of genuinely different type: the local one inspects one prime at a time
and is insensitive to total mass; the global one counts total mass and is insensitive to
individual primes.

\begin{theorem}[Incompleteness of the two-obstruction picture]\label{thm:seventy}
There exists a set $A$ of integers $\ge 2$ that is covering-free, is \emph{not}
$p$-adically separated, and is \emph{not} mass-deficient.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Take $A = \{n : n \mid 70,\ n \ge 2\} = \{2,5,7,10,14,35,70\}$. The number $70$ is weird:
its proper divisors $1,2,5,7,10,14,35$ sum to $74 > 70$, so it is abundant, yet no
sub-collection sums to exactly $70$ (a finite check over $2^7$ subsets), so it is not
pseudoperfect; Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} gives covering-freeness. Separation fails because
$2, 10 \in A$ are distinct with $v_2(2) = v_2(10) = 1 > 0$. Deficiency fails because
$74 > 70$.
\end{proof}

Thus a third mechanism operates, and $70$ is its smallest instance: enough mass, no
separation, no exact representation. The phenomenon is precisely weirdness.

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:pscol}
Let $c$ be an $r$-colouring and suppose there are a colour $i$ and a pseudoperfect $N$ such
that every divisor $n \ge 2$ of $N$ receives colour $i$. Then $c$ has a monochromatic exact
covering.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} there is an exact covering of divisors of $N$; every member is
$\ge 2$ and divides $N$, hence has colour $i$.
\end{proof}

\section{Finitisation by compactness}

\begin{definition}
For $r, N \in \mathbb{N}$, the \emph{finite Erd\H{o}s--Graham property} $\EG(r,N)$ asserts:
for every $r$-colouring $c$ there exist an exact covering $S$ and a colour $i$ with $n \le N$
and $c(n) = i$ for all $n \in S$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:mono}
$\EG(r,N)$ is monotone in $N$, and $\EG(r,N)$ implies $\EG(r)$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Compactness]\label{thm:compact}
For every $r$: \quad $\EG(r) \iff \exists N,\ \EG(r,N)$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) Proposition~\ref{prop:mono}.

($\Rightarrow$) Assume $\EG(r)$ and suppose $\EG(r,N)$ fails for every $N$. Then for each
level $N$ there is a \emph{bad colouring} $g_N$ admitting no monochromatic exact covering
with all elements $\le N$. The naive diagonal ``take $g_N$ for large $N$'' fails, since
different candidate coverings require different levels. We instead form a limit colouring
along a fixed non-principal ultrafilter $\mathcal{U}$ on the set of levels.

For each $n$ the map $N \mapsto g_N(n)$ takes values in a set of $r$ colours. Since the
finitely many fibres $\{N : g_N(n) = i\}$ cover all levels and $\mathcal U$ is an
ultrafilter, exactly one fibre lies in $\mathcal U$; define $c(n)$ to be that colour, so
that
\begin{equation}\label{eq:ae}
  \{ N : g_N(n) = c(n) \} \in \mathcal U \qquad \text{for every } n .
\end{equation}
(Finiteness of the palette is essential here.)

By $\EG(r)$ applied to $c$, there are an exact covering $S$ and a colour $i$ with $c(n)=i$
for all $n \in S$. Since $S$ is finite, \eqref{eq:ae} and closure of $\mathcal U$ under
finite intersections give
\[
  A = \{N : \forall n \in S,\ g_N(n) = c(n)\}
    = \bigcap_{n \in S}\{N : g_N(n) = c(n)\} \in \mathcal U ,
\]
and $B = \{N : \max S \le N\} \in \mathcal U$, because its complement is finite and
$\mathcal U$ is non-principal. Pick $N \in A \cap B$. Then $S$ is monochromatic for $g_N$
and all its elements are $\le N$, contradicting badness of $g_N$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}
$\EG(1,6)$ holds, witnessed by $\{2,3,6\}$, and $6$ is optimal: by
Theorems~\ref{thm:three} and~\ref{thm:unique} the unique covering of minimal size is
$\{2,3,6\}$, and no subset of $\{2,3,4,5\}$ has reciprocal sum $1$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}[A computational lower bound for two colours]\label{rem:lower}
Colour $\{2,3,\dots,55\}$ by placing
\[
  R = \{3, 4, 6, 7, 8, 10, 11, 14, 17, 20, 21, 24, 25, 27, 29, 31, 32, 33, 34, 37, 41, 45,
  46, 47, 49, 50, 52\}
\]
in one class and the complement in the other. An exhaustive exact search --- by
meet-in-the-middle over the common denominator $\lcm(R)$, and independently by depth-first
search with suffix-sum pruning --- shows that neither class contains a subset of reciprocal
sum $1$. Hence $\EG(2,N)$ fails for every $N \le 55$, and the least valid two-colour bound
exceeds $55$. This is a computational finding rather than a proved theorem, but the
certificate is small and directly checkable. Its striking feature is the mass: the two
classes have reciprocal sums approximately $1.889$ and $1.704$, nearly twice what an exact
covering needs, and still admit none --- the weird-core phenomenon of
Theorem~\ref{thm:seventy} at larger scale.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
Theorem~\ref{thm:compact} is effective in structure but not in size: the ultrafilter
argument proceeds by contradiction from failure at \emph{every} level and extracts no bound
on $N(r)$. Nevertheless it converts a conjecture about arbitrary colourings of an infinite
set into a finite, in-principle-decidable assertion. For $r = 2$, verifying $\EG(2,N)$ for a
single concrete $N$ would give a wholly finite proof of the two-colour case. The search
space of $2^{N-1}$ colourings is prohibitive by brute force but highly structured: by
Theorem~\ref{thm:2colsmall} the adversary cannot confine one colour to $\{2,\dots,9\}$, and
by Theorem~\ref{thm:pscol} he must break up every pseudoperfect divisor lattice below $N$.
\end{remark}

\section{Algorithms}

\textbf{(A1) Splitting-ladder generation.} Given $k \ge 3$, start from $\{2,3,6\}$ and
repeatedly replace the maximum $m$ by $m+1$ and $m(m+1)$ until the size is $k$. Cost:
$O(k)$ arithmetic operations, with doubly-exponential growth of the denominators
(Sylvester's sequence), so bignum arithmetic is needed beyond about $k=8$. Correctness:
Theorem~\ref{thm:splitop}.

\textbf{(A2) Exact covering search inside a divisor lattice.} Given $N$, solve the
subset-sum problem $\sum_{d\in D} d = N$ over the proper divisors of $N$ by dynamic
programming on $\{0,\dots,N\}$, then dualise via $d \mapsto N/d$. Cost:
$O(N\,\tau(N))$ time and $O(N)$ space. Correctness: Theorem~\ref{thm:duality}. Choosing
$N = 27720$ and restricting to divisors that are not prime powers reproduces
Theorem~\ref{thm:npp}; restricting to divisors $\ge 10$ reproduces Theorem~\ref{thm:ten}.

\textbf{(A3) Obstruction certification.} Given a finite set $A$, tabulate $v_p$ across $A$
for each prime $p$ dividing a member and report \emph{separated} if no positive value
repeats; then $A$ is covering-free by Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}. Cost:
$O(|A|\log \max A)$ after factoring. The test is sound but, by Theorem~\ref{thm:seventy},
incomplete.

\textbf{(A4) Greedy Egyptian expansion.} For rational $0 < x < 1$, repeatedly subtract
$1/\lceil 1/x \rceil$. The numerator strictly decreases, so the algorithm terminates: every
rational in $(0,1)$ has an Egyptian representation. This shows that representability is
easy, and that the difficulty of the colouring problem lies entirely in the constraint.

\section{Discussion and future directions}

The elementary theory of exact coverings is complete in the following sense: the realisable
sizes are exactly $\{3,4,5,\dots\}$, the minimal covering is unique, an explicit ladder
generates all larger sizes, and each covering brackets its own cardinality. On the colouring
side the pigeonhole step is unconditional and elementary, and the one-colour case has
optimal bound $6$. Theorem~\ref{thm:insuff} is the sharpest statement of the residual
difficulty: the property produced by pigeonhole is strictly weaker than the property needed.
Two structurally different obstructions explain large families of covering-free sets, and
Theorem~\ref{thm:seventy} shows that even together they are incomplete.

\begin{conjecture}[Large-minimum coverings]
For every $K$ there is an exact covering all of whose denominators exceed $K$. Equivalently,
every cofinite set of integers $\ge 2$ contains an exact covering, so the Erd\H{o}s--Graham
conjecture holds for every colouring with a cofinite colour class.
\end{conjecture}

Under the duality $d \mapsto N/d$ this asks that some $N$ be a sum of distinct divisors all
at most $N/K$ --- a ``$K$-practical'' strengthening of pseudoperfectness --- and the mass
required, $\sum_{e \mid N,\ e \ge K} 1/e \ge 1$, is available as soon as
$\sigma(N)/N > 1 + \log K$. The missing ingredient is a practical-number lemma: every
integer up to $\sigma(N)$ is a sum of distinct divisors of $N$ for $N = \lcm(1,\dots,m)$.
A $K$ with no such covering would contradict the divergence of
$\sum_{e \mid \lcm(1..m)} 1/e$.

\begin{conjecture}[Weird cores]
For every covering-free set $A$ of integers $\ge 2$ there is a finite $F \subseteq A$ with
$\sum_{n\in F} 1/n > 1$ and no subset of $F$ summing to $1$: covering-freeness is always
witnessed by a finite weird configuration.
\end{conjecture}

The number $70$ shows the local criterion is not necessary, and abundance without
pseudoperfectness is precisely weirdness; the conjecture asserts that weirdness is the only
residual mechanism. By Theorem~\ref{thm:compact} this is a statement about finite
hypergraphs. It would be refuted by a covering-free set whose finite subsets have subset-sums
arbitrarily close to $1$ from below but never above.

\begin{conjecture}[Erd\H{o}s--Graham--Rado numbers]
$\EG(2,N)$ holds for some $N \le 100$.
\end{conjecture}

By Remark~\ref{rem:lower} the least such $N$ exceeds $55$, so if the conjecture is true the
value lies in a narrow window.

By Theorem~\ref{thm:compact} the two-colour case is equivalent to the existence of some
bound; the least such bound $\EGR(2)$ is conjectured small enough for a structured search
(SAT or ILP over $2$-colourings of $\{2,\dots,N\}$, with the constraints of \S5 and \S6 as
strong initial clauses) to certify it. A refutation for a given $N$ is a single explicit bad
$2$-colouring of $\{2,\dots,N\}$, easy to check. The quantity $\EGR(r)$ is defined by the
same recipe and is finite for every $r$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:compact} together with Croot's
theorem.

Further lines: determine $\EGR(2)$ exactly or bound it; quantify the density of
pseudoperfect numbers usable in Theorem~\ref{thm:pscol}; classify covering-free divisor
lattices, which by Theorem~\ref{thm:duality} is exactly the classification of
non-pseudoperfect numbers into the deficient and the weird; and seek a quantitative form of
the local obstruction bounding how many elements of a covering must share the maximal
$p$-power.

\section{Conclusion}

Exact Egyptian coverings are simultaneously abundant and rigid. They exist in every size
from three upward, are generated by a single explicit doubling move, and can avoid all prime
powers and all small denominators --- yet they are forbidden entirely inside pairwise coprime
families, inside the primes and prime powers, and inside the divisor lattice of any deficient
or weird number. The colouring conjecture of Erd\H{o}s and Graham sits exactly at the tension
point between these facts: the easy argument produces mass, and mass provably is not enough.
What is mapped here is the shape of the gap.

